- Live source review confirmed Java 5.5.1 creates its outbound `SslHandler` without a peer hostname. The Java probe therefore records acceptance of a CA-trusted wrong-host certificate, while Rust rejects it as required by the Phase 3 TLS plan; both clients accept the trusted CA and reject an untrusted CA. The inventory, Phase 3 English/Traditional Chinese plan, scenario assertions, local README, server results, compatibility summary, owner handoff, and release checklist now describe this observed difference.
- Clean-source run `20261025T113910Z` passed on `d673e6ff6aefcd12831203a5b131c72a37c22678`, with `source_code_dirty=false`, `exit_status=0`, and 90 seconds. Java and Rust raw NameServer route results matched. Client TLS used an ephemeral local TLS-terminating proxy that forwarded RocketMQ traffic to the isolated plain 5.3.4 Docker Broker; it is not Broker-native TLS evidence. All Java/Rust clients reported zero pending requests, retained channels, and worker tasks.
- The scenario deleted `compat_p3_tls` and removed its Compose containers, network, and volume. The archived synthetic CA/proxy private keys were deleted after the run; public certificates and TLS accept/reject logs remain. The three preceding failed attempts are retained as diagnostics; the manifest points only to the passing run.
